/* Built-in reference T&EOs (publicly releasable). Imported T&EOs are added at runtime. */
const TRACE_BUILTIN_TEOS=[{"id":"63-BDE-1060","code":"63-BDE-1060","title":"Coordinate Finance Support Operations","version":"20 Mar 2024","source":"63-BDE-1060 (Coordinate Finance Support Operations).pdf","sourceType":"Official publicly releasable T&EO (demonstration reference)","echelonNote":"Source T&EO is not confirmed as an assigned unit METL task.","conditions":"The command headquarters (HQs) and subordinate units/elements are deployed to provide sustainment support to Army/Joint/ Multinational forces in an assigned area ofÂ operations duringÂ large scale combat operations (LSCO) across multiple domains.Â The sustainment brigade has the mission to coordinate finance operations with the supporting finance unit / agency.Â Higher and supported command mission execution information is available on the network or in hard copy format. SustainmentÂ information systems and network capabilities enable staff elements to review and update the Common Operational Picture (COP).Â As required, the supporting finance unit(s) such as a Finance Battalion (FIBN), Finance Companies (FICOs), finance teams, or designated finance agencies are providing required financial support.Â As required based on the supported unitsâ operations, the supporting finance unit has or will position subordinate unit(s) throughout the supported unit area of responsibility (AOR) to ensure timely, sufficient, and enduring financial support, staff elements and / or subordinate units.Â When required, these supporting financial units collaborate with strategic support area supporting strategic agencies, intermediate staging bases (ISBs), and forward operating sustainment support units.ï»¿Â The organization is in a dynamic and complex operational environment, with a replicated Live, Virtual, Constructive and/or Gaming (LVC-G) regular, conventional or hybrid threat (BDE or Division size OPFOR), with near-peer enablers that should include cyber, degraded space, EW, advanced ISR, C3D, integrated air defense, counter and precision fires, SOF, CBRNE considerations, information warfare, and air threats.ï»¿ Enemy offensive electronic warfare (EW), counterspace, and offensive cyber capabilities are trying to jam, spoof, exploit, or destroy friendly space based reconnaissance and communications platforms to prevent effective friendly mission command and ISR. Enemy is using long-range strike capabilities against civilian infrastructure and resources that support military operations, such as transportation networks, energy generation and distribution systems, and the defense industrial base. Some iterations of this task should be performed in MOPP 4.","standard":"The brigade staff coordinates finance operations throughout the AO in accordance with FM 1-06, standard operating procedures (SOP), no later than time prescribed in OPORD, the commander's intent, and orders from higher headquarters.ï»¿Â","steps":[{"number":1,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":true,"text":"Upon receipt of mission, the commander / OIC directs the staff to begin the operation process to coordinate finance support operations within the operational area.","details":["a. Alert staff and other key participants.","b. Gather tools, e.g. applicable doctrinal manuals, OPLAN/OPORDs, and current running estimates.","c. Verify and update running estimate.","d. The commander and staff conduct an initial assessment of time and resources available to plan finance support.","e. The commander provides intent, initial planning guidance, and commander’s critical information requirements (CCIRs) to assist in developing an understanding of the operational environment (OE) in support of the higher headquarters’ objectives."],"page":5},{"number":2,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"The applicable staff begins mission analysis of the higher headquarters’ plan and/or order in order to gain share mutual understanding:","details":["a. The mission of the higher headquarters’ two echelons above the unit.","b. The higher headquarters’—","(1) Commander’s intent.","(2) Mission.","(3) Available assets.","(4) Timeline.","c. The designate support areas where financial support operations will occur.","d. The missions of adjacent, supporting, and supported units and their relationships to the higher headquarters’ plan.","e. The missions or goals of unified action partners that work in the operational areas."],"page":5},{"number":3,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The S-2 staff, along with other applicable staff, conduct / update Intelligence Preparation of the Operational Environment (IPOE):","details":["a. Define the operational environment.","b. Describe effects on operations.","c. Evaluate the threat.","d. Develop threat course of action."],"page":5},{"number":4,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The Support Operations staff conduct / update sustainment preparation of the operating environment (SPoOE) data related to conducting finance support operations:","details":["a. Infrastructure: Identify infrastructure and capacity to conduct finance operations.","b. Resources: Identify resources within the operational environment.","c. Geography & Services: Gather information on climate, terrain, flooding, precipitation, and endemic diseases in the area of operation (AO)."],"page":5},{"number":5,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with the supported unit or higher headquarters financial staff; the supporting unit staff, such as the brigade S8 staff, Deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCOs, and / or designated staff with supporting finance unit / agency, conduct course of action development (COADEV) to plan for financial support operations:","details":["a. Develop financial support assessment as it applies to operations.","b. Identify, certify, and manage specific financial support requirements.","c. The staff, led by the S-3, conduct a risk assessment to identify and assess hazards that may adversely impact financial support operations."],"page":5},{"number":6,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Based on the mission analysis efforts, the staff conducts applicable COA Development, COA Analysis, COA Comparison, and COA Approval for retrograde operations:","details":["a. Conduct COA Development, Key Outputs:","(1) Review the priorities set by the supported commander.","(2) Commander's selected COA(s) with COA statement and sketch.","(3) Commander's refined planning guidance to include COA analysis guidance and evaluation criteria.","(4) Updated running estimates, IPOE, and assumptions.","(5) Ensure that the controls and risk reduction measures developed.","b. Conduct COA Analysis, Key Outputs:","(1) Refined COA(s).","(2) Draft Decision Support Tool (DST) and Decision Support Matrix (DSM).","(3) COA synchronization matrix or set of sketch notes.","(4) Identification of potential branches and sequels.","(5) Updated running estimates and assumptions. ","c. Conduct COA Comparison, Key Outputs:","(1) Staff-recommended COA.","(2) Cost and benefits between COAs.","(3) COA selection rationale.","(4) Updated running estimates and assumptions.","d. Obtain COA Approval, Key Products:","(1) Approved COA with any modifications.","(2) Commander's final planning guidance.","(3) Refined commander's intent, CCIRs, and EEFIs."],"page":5},{"number":7,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with the supported unit staff; the supporting staff members, such as the G8, deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCOs and / or designated officer / NCO provide finance support operations input to the applicable WARNORD, OPORD, FRAGORD and annexes:","details":["a. Paragraph 4 - Sustainment. Identify and list sustainment priorities for intelligence key tasks and specify additional sustainment instructions as necessary, to include contractor support. Refer to Annex F (Sustainment) as required.","(1) b. (U) Personnel. Identify intelligence unique personnel requirements and concerns, including global sourcing support and contracted linguist requirements. Use subparagraphs to identify priorities and specific instructions for human resources support, financial management, legal support, and religious support. Refer to Annex F (Sustainment) as required.","(2) d. (U) Financial Management. Refer to Annex F (Sustainment) as required.","b. ANNEX F (SUSTAINMENT) TO OPERATION PLAN or ORDER:","(1) Personnel. Provide personnel information. Outline plans for unit-strength maintenance; personnel management; morale development and maintenance; discipline, law, and order; headquarters management; force provider; religious support; and legal and finance support. Post personnel services unit locations to the logistic synchronization matrix and the overlay at Tab A (Sustainment Overlay) to Appendix 1 (Logistics) to Annex F (Sustainment). Refer to Appendix 2 (Personnel Services Support) to Annex F (Sustainment) as required.","(2) (2) (U) Financial Management. Provide financial management support information. Refer to Tab B (Financial Management) to Appendix 2 (Personnel Services Support) to Annex F (Sustainment) as required.","(3) Appendix 4 (Financial Management) of Annex F (Sustainment)."],"page":6},{"number":8,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In support of financial support operations, the staff initiates applicable information collection efforts in order to answer identified information requirements, such as commander's critical information requirements (CCIR) and essential elements of friendly information (EEFI).","details":[],"page":6},{"number":9,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"The brigade staff, such as the S8, deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCO, or designated officer / NCO initiate applicable financial support operations with supported unit and the supporting finance HQ / staff, such as the Finance Support Center, Finance Operations Center, Finance BN, or finance agencies.","details":["a. Establish financial reporting requirements and times for the command. This includes data analytics for senior leader decision-making and metric reporting.","b. Determine currency requirements and replenishment (U.S. and foreign) needs, receiving collections, making payments on certified vouchers, supporting detainee operations, safeguarding funds, and protecting funds from fraud, waste, and abuse.","c. Establish a common operational picture (COP).","d. Establish liaison between sustainment forces and supported forces."],"page":6},{"number":10,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"If required, the S6 staff initiate network preparations, confirms information systems are functional with connectivity.","details":["a. Maintain automated system and communications support.","b. Provide technical support and guidance for operations of information systems."],"page":6},{"number":11,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"The S8 staff, deputy SPO, assigned finance officer / NCO, or supporting finance unit / agency conduct pre-operations checks and inspection of financial support operations.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":12,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"As required, the S3 staff complete the financial support operations task organization.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":13,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with higher HQ or supported unit G3 staff, the supporting unit G3 staff initiate applicable security operations in support of financial support operations.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":14,"stage":"Execute","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Based on the supported unit operations, in coordination with the supporting finance unit(s) / agency, the brigade staff coordinate finance support operations:","details":["a. Integrate all financial operation support requirements into the operational planning.","b. In coordination with the supporting finance unit / agency, contribute to development of the finance concept of support.","c. Within the supported unit concept of support, coordinate the movement of supporting finance unit(s)."],"page":6},{"number":15,"stage":"Execute","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with the supporting finance center, unit(s), or agency, the designated brigade staff updates financial support operations running estimates in order to update the common operating picture (COP).","details":[],"page":6},{"number":16,"stage":"Execute","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Within the unit battle rhythm events, the brigade staff monitor and report status of applicable financial support operations to the commander / OIC, higher HQ, or supported unit staff.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":17,"stage":"Execute","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with the supporting finance unit (s) / agency and the G6 / S6 staff, the SASMO staff confirm applicable sustainment automation and administrative procedures are joint and Army compliant.","details":["a. Identify systems and software needed for employment within AOR.","b. Install and update hardware and software to enable operational and tactical finance operations.","c. Supervise system integrity and ensure appropriate security measures for automated equipment hardware, software, and data are in place."],"page":6},{"number":18,"stage":"Assess","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"The commander and the staff use running estimates to continuously assess the current situation to determine if the financial support operation is going according to plan and whether or not the plan is going to accomplish the commander’s intent.","details":[],"page":7},{"number":19,"stage":"Assess","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Applicable staff OIC conduct finance support operations AARs.   operations, during movement, or Soldier duties as assigned. ","details":["b. Assess the Hazard. Analyze potential severity of environmental degradation using the Environmental Risk Assessment. Severity of environmental degradation is considered when determining the potential effect an operation will have on the environment. The risk impact value is defined as an indicator of the severity of environmental degradation. Quantify the risk to the environment resulting from the operation as extremely high, medium, or low, using the environmental risk assessment matrixes. ","c. Make Environmental Risk Decisions. Make decisions and develop measures to reduce high environmental risks. ","d. Brief Chain of Command. Brief chain of command (to include installation environmental office, if applicable), on proposed plans and pertinent high-risk environmental matrixes. Risk decisions are made at a level of command that corresponds to the degree of risk.  Reference: GTA 05-08-002, Environmental-Related Risk Assessment "],"page":7}],"measures":["Upon receipt of mission, the commander / OIC directs the staff to begin the operation process to coordinate finance support operations within the operational area.","The applicable staff begins mission analysis of the higher headquarters’ plan and/or order in order to gain share mutual understanding:","The S-2 staff, along with other applicable staff, conduct / update Intelligence Preparation of the Operational Environment (IPOE):","The Support Operations staff conduct / update sustainment preparation of the operating environment (SPoOE) data related to conducting finance support operations:","In coordination with the supported unit or higher headquarters financial staff; the supporting unit staff, such as the brigade S8 staff, Deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCOs, and / or designated staff with supporting finance unit / agency, conduct course of action development (COADEV) to plan for financial support operations:","Based on the mission analysis efforts, the staff conducts applicable COA Development, COA Analysis, COA Comparison, and COA Approval for retrograde operations:","In coordination with the supported unit staff; the supporting staff members, such as the G8, deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCOs and / or designated officer / NCO provide finance support operations input to the applicable WARNORD, OPORD, FRAGORD and annexes:","In support of financial support operations, the staff initiates applicable information collection efforts in order to answer identified information requirements, such as commander's critical information requirements (CCIR) and essential elements of friendly information (EEFI).","The brigade staff, such as the S8, deputy SPO, assigned finance officers / NCO, or designated officer / NCO initiate applicable financial support operations with supported unit and the supporting finance HQ / staff, such as the Finance Support Center, Finance Operations Center, Finance BN, or finance agencies.","If required, the S6 staff initiate network preparations, confirms information systems are functional with connectivity.","The S8 staff, deputy SPO, assigned finance officer / NCO, or supporting finance unit / agency conduct pre-operations checks and inspection of financial support operations.","As required, the S3 staff complete the financial support operations task organization.","In coordination with higher HQ or supported unit G3 staff, the supporting unit G3 staff initiate applicable security operations in support of financial support operations.","Based on the supported unit operations, in coordination with the supporting finance unit(s) / agency, the brigade staff coordinate finance support operations:","In coordination with the supporting finance center, unit(s), or agency, the designated brigade staff updates financial support operations running estimates in order to update the common operating picture (COP).","Within the unit battle rhythm events, the brigade staff monitor and report status of applicable financial support operations to the commander / OIC, higher HQ, or supported unit staff.","In coordination with the supporting finance unit (s) / agency and the G6 / S6 staff, the SASMO staff confirm applicable sustainment automation and administrative procedures are joint and Army compliant.","The commander and the staff use running estimates to continuously assess the current situation to determine if the financial support operation is going according to plan and whether or not the plan is going to accomplish the commander’s intent.","Applicable staff OIC conduct finance support operations AARs.   operations, during movement, or Soldier duties as assigned. "]},{"id":"63-BDE-4912","code":"63-BDE-4912","title":"Conduct Distribution Operations (DSB)","version":"24 Apr 2024","source":"63-BDE-4912 (Conduct Distribution Operations).pdf","sourceType":"Official publicly releasable T&EO (demonstration reference)","echelonNote":"Source T&EO is not confirmed as an assigned unit METL task.","conditions":"The brigade receives an OPORD toÂ conductÂ distribution operations in support of combat operations. The sustainment support area is established. The staffÂ has been directed to conduct distribution operations in support of concept of operations by planning, coordinating, and synchronizing sustainment capability assets within the area of operations (AO) distribution supply pipeline, to include total asset visibility / in-transit visibility (TAV/ITV) of the commodities; movements; and units within, units assigned, units in or out bound from the area of responsibility.Â The brigade has communications with higher, adjacent, subordinate, and supporting elements.Â The unit is in aÂ dynamic and complex operational environment, with a replicated Live, Virtual, Constructive and/or Gaming (LVC-G) regular, conventional or hybrid threat (BDE or Division size OPFOR), with near-peer enablers that should include cyber, degraded space, EW, advanced ISR, C3D, integrated air defense, counter and precision fires, SOF, CBRNE considerations, information warfare, and air threats. All operational variables of PMESII-PT must be present to various degrees to stimulate responses for key training objectives. Enemy offensive electronic warfare (EW), counterspace, and offensive cyber capabilities are trying to jam, spoof, exploit, or destroy friendly space- based reconnaissance and communications platforms to prevent effective friendly mission command and ISR. Enemy is using long-range strike capabilities against civilian infrastructure and resources that support military operations, such as transportation networks, energy generation and distribution systems, and the defense industrial base. Threat capabilities may cover a wide spectrum, including information gathering; use of hostile force sympathizers; use of terrorist activities (such as suicide bombings); and reinforced squad operations in a chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear (CBRN) environment.ï»¿ Some iterations of this task should be performed in MOPP 4.","standard":"The staff conductsÂ Âdistribution operationsÂ Âin accordance with established operational timelines, SOP, the commander's guidance, and ATP 4-91Â (Division Sustainment Operations).","steps":[{"number":1,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"Upon receipt of mission the commander / OIC provides initial planning guidance, staff gather required tools, and update applicable running estimates. ","details":["a. Alert staff and other key participants.","b. Provide guidance for distribution operations in the operational area.","c. Gather tools, e.g. applicable doctrinal manuals; OPLAN/OPORDs; Current Running Estimates, such as Sustainment Preparation of Operating Environment (SPoOE) files.","d. Command staff conduct initial assessment to update running estimates.","e. Staff determine key outputs, such as Commander's initial guidance; Initial allocation of time; and publish associated Warning Order."],"page":5},{"number":2,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"Led by the DCO, XO, or S3, the SPO staff with support of other applicable staff conduct distribution operations mission analysis:","details":["a. The staff analyzes the higher headquarters' plan or order.","b. Determine the missions of adjacent, supporting, and supported units and their relationships to higher headquarters' plan.","c. Staff evaluates facts and assumptions for distribution operations in support of concept of operations:","(1) Assess materiel management functions:","(a) Determine and validate materiel requirements for distribution to supported units.","(b) Obtain materiel to be distributed by appropriate commodity and quantity.","(c) Coordinate distribution according to command priorities.","(2) Assess transportation functions:","(a) Determine and validate best mode of transportation for distribution to supported units.","(b) Identify units with adequate modes to meet distribution requirements.","(c) Deliver this information to the distribution integrators.","(3) Assess distribution integration functions:","(a) Develop a distribution plan based on distribution requirements and available transportation.","(b) Integrate the information provided by the materiel managers to coordinate with the transportation managers to allocate transportation by commodity, quantity, priority, and recommended mode."],"page":5},{"number":3,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The S-2 staff, along with other applicable staff, conduct / update Intelligence Preparation of the Operational Environment (IPOE):","details":["a. Define the operational environment.","b. Describe effects on operations.","c. Evaluate the threat.","d. Develop threat course of action.","e. Draft initial priority intelligence requirements (PIR).","f. Modify combined obstacle overlay.","g. List threat intelligence collection capabilities by extracting information from higher command's security estimate.","h. List essential elements of friendly information (EEFI) from higher headquarters' security estimate.","i. List indicators which affect or may compromise the EEFI.","j. List countermeasure requirements by reviewing the higher HQ security estimate and the command's operations profile."],"page":5},{"number":4,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The support operations officer (SPO) staff conduct / update sustainment preparation of the operating environment (SPoOE) data related to distribution operations:","details":["a. Infrastructure: Identify infrastructure and capacity to receive personnel, commodities, equipment and other resources.","b. Resources: Identify resources within the operational environment.","c. Geography & Services: Gather information on climate, terrain, precipitation, and endemic diseases in the AO to determine equipment requirements."],"page":5},{"number":5,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"With support of applicable staff sections, the support operations staff conducts mission analysis to identify distribution operations requirements, capabilities, and shortfalls with mitigations: + a. Based on the supported unit concept of operation with associated priorities of support, analyze applicable materiel management areas:","details":["(1) requirements","(2) capabilities","(3) shortfalls + b. Based on the supported unit concept of operation with associated priorities of support, analyze transportation operations:","(1) requirements","(2) capabilities","(3) shortfalls + c. Based on the supported unit concept of operation, analyze the applicable distribution networks of physical, information, communication, and financial:","(1) requirements","(2) capabilities","(3) shortfalls"],"page":5},{"number":6,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"Designated staff conducts course of action development activities to develop distribution plans in support of the supported unit concept of operations.","details":["a. Analyzes projected sustainment requirements and capabilities of subordinate logistics, financial management, human resources, and combat-health support (CHS) supporting units.","b. Analyzes TAV/ITV distribution information.","c. Develops the supported command's battlefield distribution scheme in concert with higher command-levels' supporting units.","d. Recommends tactical disposition of sustainment assets to the commander.","e. Conduct applicable sustainment analysis to select course of action.","f. Develop commodity distribution concepts.","g. Determine force protection requirements.","h. Plan for retrograde.","i. Establish command and support relationships.","j. Establish task organization that employs transportation assets in support of the distribution operations.","k. Establish the priorities of support for transportation before, during and after the distribution operation.","l. Synchronize with the supported headquarters operation and across all warfighting functions."],"page":6},{"number":7,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Conduct COA Analysis, Key Outputs:","details":["a. Assess the status of all sustainment functions, determine the feasibility of each COA, determines critical requirements for each function, and identify potential problems and deficiencies.","b. Review refined COA(s).","c. Develop Draft Decision Support Tool (DST) and Decision Support Matrix (DSM).","d. Develop COA synchronization matrix or set of sketch notes.","e. Identification of potential branches and sequels.","f. Refine task organization.","g. Update running estimates and assumptions."],"page":6},{"number":8,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Conduct COA Comparison, Key Outputs:","details":["a. Provide staff-recommended COA.","b. Advantages and disadvantages between COAs.","c. COA selection rationale.","d. Update running estimates and assumptions."],"page":6},{"number":9,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Obtain COA Approval, Key Products:","details":["a. Approved COA with any modifications.","b. Commander's final planning guidance.","c. Refined commander's intent, CCIRs, and EEFIs."],"page":6},{"number":10,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The S-3 staff in coordination with the SPO staff, publishes applicable OPORD/FRAGORD covering the distribution operation concept, such as the distribution plans with associated task organization and mobility plans.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":11,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"In support of distribution operations, the staff initiates applicable information collection efforts in order to answer identified information requirements, such as commander's critical information requirements (CCIR) and essential elements of friendly information (EEFI).","details":["a. The commander and / or S3 will direct the information collection operations, such as reconnaissance operations of distribution nodes.","b. Through the information collection, the commander and staffs continuously plan, task and employ collection assets and forces to collect timely and accurate information to assist in satisfy CCIRs and other information requirements."],"page":6},{"number":12,"stage":"Prepare","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The staff initiates applicable sustainment preparations to improve the ability to execute distribution operations.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":13,"stage":"Prepare","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The staff initiate Information Network preparations, such as:","details":["a. Management of available bandwidth; along with availability and location of data and information.","b. Positioning and structure of network assets.","c. Tracking status of key network systems.","d. Arraying sensors and the information network to support the concept of the operation."],"page":6},{"number":14,"stage":"Prepare","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The commander and staff conduct a rehearsal to synchronize the distribution operations with other units to create a common understanding of the distribution operations.","details":[],"page":7},{"number":15,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Commander/Officer in charge complete task organizing their force to obtain the right mix of capabilities and expertise to accomplish the mission.","details":[],"page":7},{"number":16,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The commander and staff execute battle rhythm events to synchronize current and future distribution operations such as Movement Boards, Distribution Boards, Battle Update Briefs (BUB), and Commander Update Briefs (CUB).","details":["a. The logistics battle rhythm activities must be sequenced in a logistical order so that the outputs of one activity informs the inputs of another activity.","b. The Support Operation staff conducts a daily Logistics Synchronization Meeting (LOGSYNC) to validate and consolidate logistics requirements.","c. Key Leaders attendance should include but not limited to the following, such as the BDE S4, BDE S4, BDE S1, BN XOs, BN S1s, BN S4s, BDE medical planners, BDE Medical supply officer, BN MEDOs, SPO, Support BN S2, Support BNS3, Support BN S6, Transportation Officer, Ammunition Officer, Food Service Officer, SASMO and SSA representative.","d. LOGSYNC Meeting agenda","(1) Enemy situation update","(2) Upcoming mission","(3) Review LOGSTATS to validate logistics requirements by time, space and location for distribution.","(4) Review sustainment operations for the next 24 to 72 hours.","(5) Coordinate with the supporting MEB or local terrain manager to obtain convoy escorts or establish a movement a corridor that provides for coordinated responses to level I and II threats.","(6) Coordinate the movement plan.","(7) Review SASMO status."],"page":7},{"number":17,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The commander and staff synchronize current and future distribution operations with supported command’s concept of operations and across all warfighting functions.","details":["a. The staff maintains the common operational picture (COP).","b. The S3 employs intelligence ,surveillance and reconnaissance assets to provide situational understanding/awareness for the coordination of the distribution operations.","c. The S3 monitors logistics package(LOGPAC) operations to ensure that required quantity of each class of supply are delivered to supported units IAW logistics synchronization matrix.","d. The SPO monitors maneuver based triggers to relocate sustainment assets as required during the distribution operations.","e. The transportation officer manages transportation flow capacity by maintaining visibility of resources that are being transshipped at or transiting nodes during the distribution operations.","f. The transportation officer manages containers, flat racks, pallets and trailers in the Area of Operation.","g. The SPO or designated brigade staff officer establishes a flat rack control point at or near supply point to facilitate seamless flat rack exchange and accountability.","h. The SPO or designated brigade staff officer oversees retrograde operations for all classes of supply sent back to the sustainment brigade from the support battalions and supported units in the distribution operations.","i. The staff provides logistics support updates and their impact on current/future distribution operations to the brigade commander and higher Headquarters as required using analog and digital communications."],"page":7},{"number":18,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The applicable SPO staff manage distribution node and mode operations in accordance with commander's intent, applicable orders, and / or SOPs.","details":["a. Coordinate internally to manage distribution pipeline volume, flow rates, and content in and out bound to subordinate supporting units/elements.","b. Manage the flow of multi- consignee shipments in and out bound to the subordinate supporting units/elements.","c. The brigade support operations staff maintains liaison with the higher headquarters and supported units/elements to ensure they are in sync with the preparation process."],"page":7},{"number":19,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The SPO materiel managers coordinates resupply for each applicable class of supply.","details":["a. The staff update applicable running estimates.","b. The applicable SPO staff participates in the movement boards to ensure inbound sustainment movements, internal sustainment movement, and supporting coordination are synchronized and forecasted."],"page":7},{"number":20,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"In coordination with the Distribution Integration Branch staff, the transportation staff and the materiel managers coordinate distribution operations from the source of supply / service to the point of need.","details":["a. Manages distribution terminal operations in coordination with the subordinate supporting units.","b. Coordinates internally to manage distribution pipeline volume, flow rates, and contents in or out bound to the subordinate supporting units.","c. Coordinates internally to manage ITV of retrograde requirements to include noncombatant evacuation order (NEO), enemy prisoner of war (EPW), mortuary affairs activities, and medical evacuation.","d. Manages the flow of multi-consignee shipments in or out bound to the subordinate supporting units.","e. Coordinates with the appropriate movement control, maintenance, general supply, PBO-Class VII, procurement, and medical materiel staff managers to manage inbound and outbound transportation with sustainment resources retrograde requirements.","f. De-conflicts unit competition for distribution resources."],"page":7},{"number":21,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"Distribution Integration Branch (DIB) staff monitors classes of supply distribution to their final destination.","details":["a. Class I,II,III, III(P), IV, VII, IX and water staff elements.","b. Field Services staff elements (particularly as related to airdrop of supplies).","c. Ground maintenance staff elements (as related to back haul/reverse logistics activities).","d. Review priorities of movement and support.","e. Determine transportation capabilities.","f. Balance transportation requirements against transportation capabilities.","g. Include a movement plan that synchronize resources with requirements, applies movement control measures, and mitigates shortfalls by applying established priorities."],"page":8},{"number":22,"stage":"Assess","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Based on the distribution operations plan, the S3 and SPO staffs assess the coordination of the distribution operations.","details":["a. To ensure that logistics services contribute to the desired effects of the subordinate units.","b. To ensure that applicable personnel services contribute to the desired effects of the subordinate unit commander.","c. To synchronize supported subordinate unit commanders' requirements with distribution capabilities.","d. To track customers requisitions, items and commodities from the supplier to their final destination through the use of applicable information systems."],"page":8},{"number":23,"stage":"Assess","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Based on the assessment, if required, FRAGORDs are published to update the distribution operations plan.   MOPP 4 Statement: Some iterations of this task should be performed in Mission-Oriented Protective Posture (MOPP) Level 1-4 as directed by the commander and/or leaders. At MOPP4, performance degradation factors increase planning completion times. Ensure to comply with commanders’ guidance and unit SOP when conducting operations in MOPP gear.  Chemical protective clothing ensemble and field protective mask restrict movement and activities. Wear appropriate MOPP gear only when threat forces have used Chemical, Biological, Radiological, and Nuclear (CBRN) weapons or as command directed. MOPP gear should be worn during CBRN training exercises.  During MOPP training, leaders must ensure personnel are monitored for potential heat and cold weather injuries. Command policies, Army regulation, and unit SOP must be followed during times of increased heat category in order to avoid heat related injury. Consider the MOPP work/rest cycles and water replacement guidelines in accordance with CBRN and Army regulations.  NVG Statement: Night vision goggles are not required to conduct this task. However, they may be required when conducting sustainment unit operations, during movement, or Soldier duties as assigned. ","details":[],"page":8}],"measures":["Upon receipt of mission the commander / OIC provides initial planning guidance, staff gather required tools, and update applicable running estimates. ","Led by the DCO, XO, or S3, the SPO staff with support of other applicable staff conduct distribution operations mission analysis:","The S-2 staff, along with other applicable staff, conduct / update Intelligence Preparation of the Operational Environment (IPOE):","The support operations officer (SPO) staff conduct / update sustainment preparation of the operating environment (SPoOE) data related to distribution operations:","With support of applicable staff sections, the support operations staff conducts mission analysis to identify distribution operations requirements, capabilities, and shortfalls with mitigations: + a. Based on the supported unit concept of operation with associated priorities of support, analyze applicable materiel management areas:","Designated staff conducts course of action development activities to develop distribution plans in support of the supported unit concept of operations.","Conduct COA Analysis, Key Outputs:","Conduct COA Comparison, Key Outputs:","Obtain COA Approval, Key Products:","The S-3 staff in coordination with the SPO staff, publishes applicable OPORD/FRAGORD covering the distribution operation concept, such as the distribution plans with associated task organization and mobility plans.","In support of distribution operations, the staff initiates applicable information collection efforts in order to answer identified information requirements, such as commander's critical information requirements (CCIR) and essential elements of friendly information (EEFI).","The staff initiates applicable sustainment preparations to improve the ability to execute distribution operations.","The staff initiate Information Network preparations, such as:","The commander and staff conduct a rehearsal to synchronize the distribution operations with other units to create a common understanding of the distribution operations.","Commander/Officer in charge complete task organizing their force to obtain the right mix of capabilities and expertise to accomplish the mission.","The commander and staff execute battle rhythm events to synchronize current and future distribution operations such as Movement Boards, Distribution Boards, Battle Update Briefs (BUB), and Commander Update Briefs (CUB).","The commander and staff synchronize current and future distribution operations with supported command’s concept of operations and across all warfighting functions.","The applicable SPO staff manage distribution node and mode operations in accordance with commander's intent, applicable orders, and / or SOPs.","The SPO materiel managers coordinates resupply for each applicable class of supply.","In coordination with the Distribution Integration Branch staff, the transportation staff and the materiel managers coordinate distribution operations from the source of supply / service to the point of need.","Distribution Integration Branch (DIB) staff monitors classes of supply distribution to their final destination.","Based on the distribution operations plan, the S3 and SPO staffs assess the coordination of the distribution operations.","Based on the assessment, if required, FRAGORDs are published to update the distribution operations plan.   MOPP 4 Statement: Some iterations of this task should be performed in Mission-Oriented Protective Posture (MOPP) Level 1-4 as directed by the commander and/or leaders. At MOPP4, performance degradation factors increase planning completion times. Ensure to comply with commanders’ guidance and unit SOP when conducting operations in MOPP gear.  Chemical protective clothing ensemble and field protective mask restrict movement and activities. Wear appropriate MOPP gear only when threat forces have used Chemical, Biological, Radiological, and Nuclear (CBRN) weapons or as command directed. MOPP gear should be worn during CBRN training exercises.  During MOPP training, leaders must ensure personnel are monitored for potential heat and cold weather injuries. Command policies, Army regulation, and unit SOP must be followed during times of increased heat category in order to avoid heat related injury. Consider the MOPP work/rest cycles and water replacement guidelines in accordance with CBRN and Army regulations.  NVG Statement: Night vision goggles are not required to conduct this task. However, they may be required when conducting sustainment unit operations, during movement, or Soldier duties as assigned. "]},{"id":"63-CMD-0050","code":"63-CMD-0050","title":"Conduct Boards, Bureaus, Centers, Cells, and Working Groups (B2C2WG)","version":"11 Apr 2025","source":"63-CMD-0050 (Conduct Boards, Bureaus, Centers, Cells, and Working Groups (B2C2WG)).pdf","sourceType":"Official publicly releasable T&EO (demonstration reference)","echelonNote":"Source T&EO is not confirmed as an assigned unit METL task.","conditions":"The command headquarters (HQs) is deployed and responsible for chairing assigned sustainment boards, centers, meetings, and activitiesto synchronize, coordinate, and integrate governmental and non-governmental sustainment support in theater / area of operation (AO). The staff implements boards, bureaus, centers, cells, and working groups (B2C2WGs) to synchronize current and future operations to establish / maintain sustainment policies and set priorities that support the military mission / operations. These B2C2WGs provide improved sustainment support through synchronization and integration of support activities that enable effective provisioning and flow of resources. The command chief of staff (CofS),Âexecutive officer (XO), or other designated officer in charge (OIC) serves as a primary focal point for coordination on all matters pertaining to sustainment for supported units, major commands ( to include specialized commands), other services, and when applicable multinational partner forces / strategic-level organizations.ï»¿ The unit is in a dynamic and complex operational environment, with a replicated Live, Virtual, Constructive and/or Gaming (LVC-G) regular, conventionalor hybrid threat (BDE or Division size OPFOR), with near-peer enablers that should include cyber, degraded space, EW, advanced ISR, C3D, integrated air defense, counter and precision fires, SOF, CBRNE considerations, information warfare, and air threats. All operational variables of PMESII-PT must be present to various degrees to stimulate responses for key training objectives. Enemy offensive electronic warfare (EW), counterspace, and offensive cyber capabilities are trying to jam, spoof, exploit, or destroy friendly space based reconnaissance and communications platforms to prevent effective friendly mission command and ISR.Â The enemy is using long-range strike capabilities against civilian infrastructure and resources that support military operations, such as transportation networks, energy generation and distribution systems, and the defense industrial base. Threat capabilities may cover a wide spectrum, including information gathering; use of hostile force sympathizers; use of terrorist activities (such asÂthe use of improvised explosive devices); and reinforced squad operations in a chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear (CBRN) environment. Some iterations of this task should be performed in MOPP 4.","standard":"In accordance with FM 6-0, the commander's guidance, and unit standard operating procedures; the commander / officer-in-charge and staff conduct B2C2WG battle rhythm events within the prescribed timelines.Â","steps":[{"number":1,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"Upon receipt of mission, the commander / officer-in-charge (OIC) provides planning guidance to the staff for developing / updating the unit's boards, bureaus, cells, centers and working groups (B2C2WG).","details":[],"page":5},{"number":2,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The staff conducts mission analysis in order to plan for the unit's B2C2WG battle rhythm.","details":["a. Higher headquarters' battle rhythm and report requirements.","b. Subordinate headquarter(s) battle rhythm requirements.","c. The duration and intensity of the operations.","d. Integrating cells' planning requirements."],"page":5},{"number":3,"stage":"Plan","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"The staff updates running estimates to inform the unit battle rhythm and develop B2C2WG.","details":["a. The G2 staff updates the Intelligence Preparation of the Operational Environment (IPOE).","b. The G4 and DMC/SPO staff update the Sustainment Preparation of the Operational Environment.","c. Determine initial CCIRs and EEFIs."],"page":5},{"number":4,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The unit chief of staff (CofS) / executive officer (XO) leads the unit staff course of action (COA) development for unit B2C2WG battle rhythm; considering factors such as:","details":["a. Purpose, frequency, agenda, and potential decisions of each B2C2WG event.","b. Lead staff element (chair), with the associated coordinating staff members (participants).","c. Required inputs, such as from whom and when.","d. The output of one activity informs another activity's inputs.","e. The B2C2WG event placed in a logical order to influence other battle rhythm events.","f. Conduct COA Development Key Outputs","(1) COA statement and sketch","(2) Refined planning guidance to include COA analysis guidance and evaluation","(3) Updated running estimates and assumptions","g. Conduct COA Analysis Key Outputs","(1) Refined COA(s)","(2) Draft decision support tool (DST) and decision support matrix (DSM)","(3) COA synchronization matrix or sketch notes","(4) Identification of potential branches and sequels","(5) Updated running estimates and assumptions","h. Conduct COA Comparison Key Outputs","(1) Staff recommended COA","(2) Cost and benefit analysis","(3) COA selection rationale","(4) Updated running estimates and assumptions","i. Obtain COA Approval Key Outputs","(1) Approved COA with and modifications","(2) Commander's final planning guidance","(3) Refined commander's intent, CCIRs, and EEFIs","(4) Staff issues WARNO / OPORD to subordinate units"],"page":5},{"number":5,"stage":"Plan","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"The G3 staff publishes an OPORD, FRAGORD, and / or SOP with unit B2C2WG battle rhythm events.","details":["a. Purpose.","b. Frequency.","c. Composition (chair and participants).","d. Inputs and expected outputs.","e. Agenda."],"page":5},{"number":6,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Staff continue to coordinate with higher, lower, adjacent, supporting, and supported unit / agency / organization staffs:","details":["a. Send and receive liaisons.","b. Establish communication links to ensure continuous contact during execution.","c. Exchange standard operating procedures (SOPs)."],"page":5},{"number":7,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Initiate local and area security operations to be refined during applicable B2C2WG events.","details":[],"page":5},{"number":8,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Conduct protection through operational security and survivability operations and refine during applicable B2C2WG events.","details":[],"page":5},{"number":9,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Identify the position and structure of network assets.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":10,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Initiate information collection efforts to gain / maintain situational understanding in support of B2C2WG events, such as updating staff running estimates.","details":["a. Track the status of key network systems.","b. Array the sensors and information systems to support the B2C2WG events."],"page":6},{"number":11,"stage":"Prepare","critical":false,"leader":false,"text":"Initiate network preparations in support of unit B2C2WG events, such as:","details":["a. Manage available bandwidth.","b. Determine the availability and location of required data and information."],"page":6},{"number":12,"stage":"Prepare","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"Based on the unit SOP, the CofS / XO / OIC leads the staff in conducting B2C2WG event rehearsals.","details":["a. Assembles appropriate staff elements and subordinate commanders or directs digital/telephonic/video conference(s).","b. Reviews the concept of support (in coordination with designated staff elements) to ensure B2C2WG events support the logistics battle rhythm.","c. Reviews (with the staff and subordinate commanders) provisions of the command's operations plan (OPLAN)/operations order (OPORD) and supported command's sustainment annexes to validate the need for B2C2WGs.","d. Verifies that all staff members and subordinate commanders understand their roles/missions related to B2C2WG being conducted for the Area of Operations (AOR)/Joint Operations Area (JOA)."],"page":6},{"number":13,"stage":"Prepare","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The CofS / XO / OIC confirms the B2C2WG activities are logically sequenced so that the output of one activity informs another activity’s inputs.","details":[],"page":6},{"number":14,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The CofS / XO / OIC and staff executes the B2C2WG battle rhythm events in order to synchronize current and future sustainment operations.","details":["a. Boards, bureaus, cells, centers and working groups are conducted in accordance with FM 6-0, Chapter 8; with sample membership and meeting agendas in Chapter 4.","b. Typical boards found within the unit’s battle rhythm include the Operations Assessment, Plans Synchronization, Sustainment, and Targeting Boards.","c. Typical cells found within the unit's battle rhythm include functional cells by warfighting function and integrating cells such as plans, future operations, and current operations.","d. Typical working groups at division and corps headquarters scheduled in the unit’s battle rhythm include the following: Assessment, Civil Affairs Operations, Cyberspace Electromagnetic Activities, Information Collection, Information Operations, Knowledge Management, Protection, Sustainment, and Targeting Working Groups."],"page":6},{"number":15,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The Commander / CofS / XO and or designated staff element(s) participate in applicable strategic / operational level combined / joint sustainment boards, centers, cells, etc., in order to provide advice and recommendations to the supported leaders and / or staff when required, including but not limited to the following:","details":["a. Joint Logistics Operations Center (JLOC).","b. Joint Deployment and Distribution Operations Center (JDDOC).","c. Combatant Commander Logistics Procurement Support Board.","d. Joint Acquisition Requirements Board (JARB).","e. Joint Civil-Military Engineering Board (JCMEB).","f. Joint Environmental Management Board (JEMB)","g. Joint Facilities Utilization Board (JFUB).","h. Logistics Coordination Board.","i. Theater - Joint Transportation Board (T-JTB).","j. Joint Movement Center (JMC).","k. Theater Patient Movement Requirements Center (TPMRC)","l. Joint Patient Movement Requirements Center (JPMRC","m. Joint Blood Program Office (JBPO)","n. Joint Petroleum Office (JPO)","o. Subarea Petroleum Office.","p. Joint Mortuary Affairs Office (JMAO).","q. Explosive Hazards Coordination Cell (EHCC)."],"page":6},{"number":16,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"Commander, CofS, XO, or designated staff monitors / participates in B2C2WG sessions, including, but not limited to the:","details":["a. Joint Materiel Priorities and Allocation Board (JMPAB), when/if required.","b. Joint Transportation Board (JTB), when/if requried.","c. Joint Logistics Operations Center (JLOC), when/if requried.","d. Deployment and Distribution Operations Center (DDOC), when/if required.","e. Defense Health Board (DHB), when/if required.","f. Defense Medical Standardization Board (DMSB), when/if required.","g. Global Patient Movement Requirements Center (GPMRC), when/if required.","h. Armed Services Blood Program (ASBP), when/if required. "],"page":6},{"number":17,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"Based on the applicable battle rhythm events and associated running estimates, the G3 staff in coordination with the other staff sections develops / updates the supporting Common Operational Picture (COP) and / or Logistics Common Operating Picture (LOGCOP) in order to maintain situational awareness of the sustainment concept of support execution.","details":[],"page":7},{"number":18,"stage":"Execute","critical":true,"leader":false,"text":"When communication networks are disrupted, unit leaders / staff use applicable analog data tracking systems and available voice communications in accordance with unit standard operating procedures to continue mission.","details":[],"page":7},{"number":19,"stage":"Assess","critical":true,"leader":true,"text":"The CofS / XO / OIC and staff assess the B2C2WG battle rhythm events by executing assessment activities:","details":["a. Monitor B2C2WG, facts, assumptions, CCIR, and running estimates to determine if adjustments are required.","b. Monitor the applicable B2C2WG to confirm adequate capabilities are available at the point of need to meet the requirements.","c. Direct actions for change / improvement via FRAGO or OPORD.   water replacement guidelines in accordance with CBRN and Army regulations.  NVG Statement: Night vision goggles are not required to conduct this task. However, they may be required when conducting sustainment unit operations, during movement, or Soldier duties as assigned.  Environment: Environmental protection is not just the law but the right thing to do. It is a continual process and starts with deliberate planning. Always be alert to ways to protect our environment during training and missions. In doing so, you will contribute to the sustainment of our training resources while protecting people and the environment from harmful effects. Refer to the current Environmental Considerations manual and the current GTA Environmental-related Risk Assessment card.  It is the responsibility of all Soldiers and Department of the Army civilians to protect the environment from damage. Army personnel must take care of the environment; that is, practice environmental stewardship. All operations conducted on Army installations will comply with federal, state, local and host nation environmental requirements and Army regulations. Army personnel will sustain compliance at all sites in the U.S. and abroad, establishing good relationships with communities and regulators. "],"page":7}],"measures":["Upon receipt of mission, the commander / officer-in-charge (OIC) provides planning guidance to the staff for developing / updating the unit's boards, bureaus, cells, centers and working groups (B2C2WG).","The staff conducts mission analysis in order to plan for the unit's B2C2WG battle rhythm.","The staff updates running estimates to inform the unit battle rhythm and develop B2C2WG.","The unit chief of staff (CofS) / executive officer (XO) leads the unit staff course of action (COA) development for unit B2C2WG battle rhythm; considering factors such as:","The G3 staff publishes an OPORD, FRAGORD, and / or SOP with unit B2C2WG battle rhythm events.","Staff continue to coordinate with higher, lower, adjacent, supporting, and supported unit / agency / organization staffs:","Initiate local and area security operations to be refined during applicable B2C2WG events.","Conduct protection through operational security and survivability operations and refine during applicable B2C2WG events.","Identify the position and structure of network assets.","Initiate information collection efforts to gain / maintain situational understanding in support of B2C2WG events, such as updating staff running estimates.","Initiate network preparations in support of unit B2C2WG events, such as:","Based on the unit SOP, the CofS / XO / OIC leads the staff in conducting B2C2WG event rehearsals.","The CofS / XO / OIC confirms the B2C2WG activities are logically sequenced so that the output of one activity informs another activity’s inputs.","The CofS / XO / OIC and staff executes the B2C2WG battle rhythm events in order to synchronize current and future sustainment operations.","The Commander / CofS / XO and or designated staff element(s) participate in applicable strategic / operational level combined / joint sustainment boards, centers, cells, etc., in order to provide advice and recommendations to the supported leaders and / or staff when required, including but not limited to the following:","Commander, CofS, XO, or designated staff monitors / participates in B2C2WG sessions, including, but not limited to the:","Based on the applicable battle rhythm events and associated running estimates, the G3 staff in coordination with the other staff sections develops / updates the supporting Common Operational Picture (COP) and / or Logistics Common Operating Picture (LOGCOP) in order to maintain situational awareness of the sustainment concept of support execution.","When communication networks are disrupted, unit leaders / staff use applicable analog data tracking systems and available voice communications in accordance with unit standard operating procedures to continue mission.","The CofS / XO / OIC and staff assess the B2C2WG battle rhythm events by executing assessment activities:"]}];
